  "live_records": {
    "q-36787": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36787",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4⟩,$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4)-f(1,2) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4⟩-⟨1,2⟩) \\\\ ({2}^{2}+2(2)(4))-({1}^{2}+2(1)(2)) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ 15 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x+2y,2x⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2},2{c}_{1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ 15 & =⟨2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2},2{c}_{1}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ 15 & =2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1} \\\\ 15 & =6{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{4-2} & =\\frac{x-1}{2-1} \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{2} & =x-1 \\\\ y-2 & =2x-2 \\\\ y & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $6{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=15,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{c}_{1}+2(2{c}_{1}) & =15 \\\\ 6{c}_{1}+4{c}_{1} & =15 \\\\ 10{c}_{1} & =15 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=2{c}_{1}=3.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{3}{2},3).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
