    "q-106584": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106584",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={y}^{3}-xz$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩,$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={y}^{3}-xz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,-1,-1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,-1,-1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 0-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-z,3{y}^{2},-x⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-{c}_{3},3{c}_{2}^{2},-{c}_{1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =⟨-{c}_{3},3{c}_{2}^{2},-{c}_{1}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =-{c}_{3}-3{c}_{2}^{2}+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-0}{(-1)}=\\frac{z-0}{(-1)} \\\\ x & =-y=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t\\,\\,y=-t,\\,z=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=-t,\\,{c}_{3}=-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $-{c}_{3}-3{c}_{2}^{2}+{c}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(-t)-3(-t)^{2}+t & =0 \\\\ t-3{t}^{2}+t & =0 \\\\ 2t-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t(2-3t) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & ={c}_{2}={c}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(0,0,0).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{2}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-106584-a",
