    "q-130899": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130899",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+xy-3{y}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨4,2⟩,$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+xy-3{y}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨4,2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4,2)-f(0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨4,2⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\ {4}^{2}+4(2)-3(2)^{2}-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨4,2⟩ \\\\ 12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨4,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x+y,x-6y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}-6{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨4,2⟩ \\\\ 12 & =⟨2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}-6{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨4,2⟩ \\\\ 12 & =4(2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2})+2({c}_{1}-6{c}_{2}) \\\\ 12 & =10{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2} \\\\ 6 & =5{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-0}{2-0} & =\\frac{x-0}{4-0} \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $5{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}=6,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{c}_{1}-4(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}) & =6 \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-2{c}_{1} & =6 \\\\ 3{c}_{1} & =6 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}=1.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(2,1).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
